WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.480
How do you start a conversation and make friends?

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.480
I'm Chad Littlefield.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.329
I co-wrote a book called Ask Powerful Questions
Create Conversations That Matter.

00:00:07.329 --> 00:00:12.970
And in this video, I'm going to share one
of my absolute favorite methods to make conversation

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:17.620
that can be used even in the most extreme
circumstances --an elevator.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:18.860
Classically awkward scenario.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:20.840
You can use it in many other scenarios.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.460
But I want to share it in the context of using
an elevator.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.240
Because if you can make conversation happen
in an elevator, you can make conversation

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.610
happen in a whole bunch of other context.

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:37.930
I will also share 2 traps that people often
fall into when they try to make conversation.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:43.830
Let's get into it.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:48.690
Alright.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.850
I'm not a fan of fluff unless it's a sandwich.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.260
So, let's dive right in.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.640
The book is structured in this Ask Powerful
Questions pyramid with multiple different

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:04.670
skills and mindsets to be able to create conversations
that matter, I want to zoom right in on chapter

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.680
number 2 on this idea of rapport.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.530
Which Will and I define as a creating a relationship
of trust with another human being.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:19.890
And specifically, the tool that we share is
to follow your natural genuine curiosity based

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:24.430
on something that the other person is wearing,
carrying, or sharing.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:25.869
So, that could be a lot of stuff.

00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:29.170
So, imagine we're in an elevator, strangers
walked in...

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:34.030
Actually, we need a stranger.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:41.880
So, imagine that you're just trapped in an
elevator with a stranger and all you have

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.670
to create conversation is what exists in that
little metal box.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:51.369
Using this tool to follow your actual genuine
curiosity based on something that you see

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:57.140
that they're wearing, carrying, sharing, or
presenting.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:02.720
And the reason this tool is so powerful is
that even though an elevator is usually silent,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:03.720
right?

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.170
It's like a narrative that's classic awkward
elevator music playing.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.690
Usually, an elevator is silent.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.099
I would say that the conversation is already
5 paragraph deep when you walk in because

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:17.919
when this person walked out into the world
or they turned up in a zoom call, they knew

00:02:17.919 --> 00:02:19.459
that they're going to be in public.

00:02:19.459 --> 00:02:24.409
And so, they put things on their body to choose
to represent or say something in some way,

00:02:24.409 --> 00:02:25.409
right?

00:02:25.409 --> 00:02:29.819
And so, if you're in an all-gray sweatsuit
and you didn't do your hair, you're probably

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:31.760
saying, "I don't care right now", right?

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:32.959
And that's presenting something.

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:36.510
It creates an opportunity for you to be curious.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:37.949
And most of the time...

00:02:37.949 --> 00:02:40.950
Now, there's always risk when you're going
to start a conversation.

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:45.450
But most of the time, the risk here is fairly
low because they knew that they're going to

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.450
put this on.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.699
I knew that I was going to have a wedding
ring when I was shooting today.

00:02:48.699 --> 00:02:52.689
And so, if you're asking about my wedding
ring, what's the story behind my wedding ring,

00:02:52.689 --> 00:02:56.480
I would have some really interesting things
to share with you.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.450
And I told you something without even saying
a word.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:04.599
And so, based on people's necklaces or clothes
or t-shirt that they're wearing, a hat.

00:03:04.599 --> 00:03:07.209
But also, something that could be in their
surroundings all well.

00:03:07.209 --> 00:03:09.260
So, what do you see in their space?

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.010
Is there something that you can reference
or ask about?

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.249
Ask to that person but it's in that shared
space.

00:03:15.249 --> 00:03:18.889
So, there's a poster up on the wall and in
an elevator.

00:03:18.889 --> 00:03:21.989
Being able to spark a conversation based on
a poster on a wall or something that shared

00:03:21.989 --> 00:03:27.950
in space is much safer or more organic than
if you were to say...

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.989
You don't spark a conversation out of nowhere.

00:03:30.989 --> 00:03:32.709
And say like, "Hey...

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:36.310
What do you think of the game last night?"

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.310
Right?

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:38.310
It's not in the elevator.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:42.709
And so, when you bring it in, it has a much
higher potential chance of being awkward.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:45.390
So, quick example or tip to help us work better
too.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:51.219
If I were to ask my friend here a question
like "Why are you so 2-faced?", right?

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:53.059
You hear the amount of judgment in that question.

00:03:53.059 --> 00:03:54.609
I would argue that there's...

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:58.469
It feels like there's a lot of judgment in
that question because it begins with the word

00:03:58.469 --> 00:03:59.469
why.

00:03:59.469 --> 00:04:03.639
So, a really great question tend to begin
with "how" or "what" and not "why".

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:06.979
I explain that in some other videos that you
can check out.

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:12.930
But a better question might be, "Hey, what
inspired the 2-tone paint job today?"

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:17.259
You see there's a little bit less judgment
and a little bit more genuine curiosity.

00:04:17.259 --> 00:04:22.150
If you want to start really great conversations
and make friends, you want curiosity to be

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.610
high and judgment to be very low.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.229
Now, before you jump off this video and you're
like, "Cool tool!

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:28.229
Let me go practice it."

00:04:28.229 --> 00:04:34.870
Let me share 2 traps that people commonly
fall into when they try to use this idea to

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.259
build a relationship with trust and make conversation
with somebody new.

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:42.509
The first trap is that people go curiosity
shopping.

00:04:42.509 --> 00:04:44.700
And so, they look around and say, "Ohh.."

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.620
And so, Steven who's filming right now.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.520
"Steven, what a nice shirt.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:50.600
That's so beautiful", right?

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:51.699
And when I do that...

00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:53.139
That's cool.

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:55.189
But it's not going to start a conversation.

00:04:55.189 --> 00:04:56.580
It's actually a conversation ender.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:58.800
Because it's a sentence that ends with a period.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.699
And so, you have to turn your shopping or
your compliments into a question or else,

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:06.689
there is no conversation to be had.

00:05:06.689 --> 00:05:11.939
By the way, the idea is not to just to talk
about Steven's shirt or my friend's 2-tone

00:05:11.939 --> 00:05:14.080
face forever.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.169
The idea behind this tool is that when that
person answers that question even with a few

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:22.500
sentences, you're going to learn 3, 4, 5,
6 other things that you can be curious about.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.860
Because conversation is very exponential,
right?

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.680
It's going to span out from there.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:27.810
Trap number 2.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:29.210
Big words.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.539
Self-disclosure versus countertransference.

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:33.620
Oh, my goodness.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:38.830
So, self-disclosure, first of all, a conversation
without self-disclosure is actually an interrogation.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:39.830
Right?

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:44.450
So, if I'm sitting here question, question,
question, question, question, question, question,

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:48.039
question, that is not a great way to meet
someboy else.

00:05:48.039 --> 00:05:51.990
And so, being mindful of using this tool to
follow your curiosity but also empathetic

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:57.229
enough to know when it's appropriate to share
and make a little bit of self-disclosure happen

00:05:57.229 --> 00:05:59.229
between you and that person.

00:05:59.229 --> 00:06:01.629
Countertransference is a psychological term.

00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:03.870
I had a mentor who was a counseling psychologist.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.259
His name is Jeff.

00:06:05.259 --> 00:06:09.009
He's a beautiful human being and he wrote
a book about countertransference.

00:06:09.009 --> 00:06:13.520
Countertransference is basically the idea
that when in a counseling relationship, that

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:19.319
when you are in a conversation and the councilor...

00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:22.099
You say something that actually triggers the
councilor.

00:06:22.099 --> 00:06:26.030
And they start projecting potentially their
emotions onto you.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.229
And so, you want to be mindful.

00:06:28.229 --> 00:06:32.340
If you get really excited about what somebody
shared because you have some commonality with

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.340
it, right?

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.969
If you find out that they're scuba divers,
your tendency is going to be to say, "Oh,

00:06:35.969 --> 00:06:38.629
I'm a scuba diver too.

00:06:38.629 --> 00:06:40.159
Can we be friends?"

00:06:40.159 --> 00:06:42.659
And that's not the ideal to do.

00:06:42.659 --> 00:06:45.030
The ideal thing to do is "Cool, that's a commonality.

00:06:45.030 --> 00:06:46.030
That's really cool."

00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:48.820
Keep that in the D-L for a little bit.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.969
And just be curious about their favorite scuba
dive that they went on.

00:06:51.969 --> 00:06:54.229
Be curious about some of their experiences
scuba diving.

00:06:54.229 --> 00:06:59.419
And it some point, it'll make total sense
to reveal and self-disclose that you also

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:02.909
scuba dive and they'll be this beautiful connection
that happens.

00:07:02.909 --> 00:07:07.909
If you like this video, I make them because
I believe that conversation should matter

00:07:07.909 --> 00:07:10.849
in conversations that don't matter shouldn't
be had.

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:12.881
And so, Me and Will wrote a book all about
it.

00:07:12.881 --> 00:07:15.189
Feel free to go on Amazon and check it out.

00:07:15.189 --> 00:07:17.219
But I also believe information should be free.

00:07:17.219 --> 00:07:18.759
So, paper does cost money.

